  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nToday-Monday...Cold front stalls south of the area today and lingers \nacross the Straits of Florida through early this week, as strong \narea of high pressure continues across the eastern U.S. and low off \nthe Mid-Atlantic coast strengthens. This will maintain a tight \npressure gradient across the area, with breezy to windy conditions \nforecast across the region. Across the interior, northeast winds \nwill increase to 15-20 mph into late morning and through this \nafternoon, and at the coast will remain around 15-25 mph. Gusts up \nto 30-35 mph are forecast for much of the area, but along the coast \nof Volusia, Brevard and Indian River counties gusts around 35-40 mph \nare expected. The Wind Advisory has therefore been extended \nsouthward to include these areas and continues through this evening. \nNot out of the question to see occasional gusts above 35 mph from\ngradient winds or gusty passing showers across the rest of the \nregion today, but does not look frequent enough for a Wind \nAdvisory. Into Monday, winds will still remain breezy (potentially\nwindy along the coast), with northeast winds 15-20 mph, with \ngusts 25-35 mph. \n\nThis fresh to strong northeast flow will produce a number of beach \nand marine hazards, including rough surf, large breaking waves, and \na high risk of life-threatening rip currents at area beaches. \nEntering the dangerous surf is highly discouraged! Long period \nonshore swells will also likely contribute to moderate beach erosion \nthrough multiple high tide cycles. A High Risk for rip currents \nexists for area beaches and will likely continue through much of \nthis week, with a High Surf Advisory remaining in effect through \nearly this week as seas continue to build.  \n\nDrier, cooler air will continue to build southward into the region \ntoday. However, stronger NE low level winds will continue to squeeze \nwhat little moisture there is out there to generate rounds of \nisolated to scattered onshore moving showers today through Monday, \nwhich may produce gusts up to 40-45 mph. However, no storms are \nexpected as instability remains very low. Highs will remain below \nnormal in the mid to upper 70s near to north of Orlando and in the \nlow 80s south. Overnight lows will be in the 60s tonight, except \nalong the coast where onshore breeze will maintain min temps in the \n70s. Then into Monday night, may see low drop as low as the upper \n50s northwest of I-4. \n\nTuesday-Saturday...Weather pattern remains fairly unchanged into the \nmiddle of this week. A relatively tight pressure gradient continues \nbetween strong ridge across the eastern U.S., stalled front to the \nsouth and strong area of low pressure lingering off the eastern \nseaboard. However, models do indicate low level winds should relax \nsome, but still produce at least breezy N/NE winds up to 15-20 mph \neach day through much of this week. Models indicate mostly dry \nconditions through this time, but can't completely rule out some \nadditional isolated light showers pushing onshore at times. Strong \nlow off the northeast U.S. will finally get pushed northeast ahead \nof another advancing mid/upper level trough into late week. The \nstrong ridge remains cemented across the eastern United States, as \nweak low/disturbance may take form across the Caribbean. The \npressure gradient between these two features may once again tighten \ninto the weekend, with onshore winds gradually strengthening once \nagain, producing breezy to windy conditions.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to remain below normal through the period, \nwith highs in the upper 70s/low 80s and overnight lows mostly in the \n60s, except upper 50s NW of I-4.",
